1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,320
There is a star coming towards us. So how

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:05,600
close will it get, and should you worry?

3
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:09,460
This is not a guess and not a rumor. The

4
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:11,560
star has been measured, and it is heading

5
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:13,180
almost straight at the sun.

6
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:19,120
Its name is Gliese 710. In about one point

7
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:21,720
three million years, it will pass closer

8
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:24,840
to us than any star we know of. It will

9
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:28,000
not hit anything, but it will sail right

10
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:29,620
through the outer edge of our solar

11
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:30,140
system.

12
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:33,240
In this film, I will show you where that

13
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:36,260
star is now, how we found out how close it

14
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:39,160
will come, what it will pass through, and

15
00:00:39,220 --> 00:00:41,240
what our night sky will look like when it

16
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:41,860
arrives.

17
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:46,040
Right now, Gliese 710 is about 62

18
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:48,420
light-years away in the constellation of

19
00:00:48,540 --> 00:00:50,100
Serpens, the serpent.

20
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:53,680
It is an orange dwarf, a little smaller

21
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:56,620
and cooler than the sun, with about 60% of

22
00:00:56,660 --> 00:00:57,540
the sun's mass.

23
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:01,260
You cannot see it. It is far too faint for

24
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:03,580
your eyes on even the darkest night.

25
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:06,520
You would need binoculars just to find it.

26
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:09,000
So why does it matter?

27
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:11,640
Because of the way it moves.

28
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:15,520
Most stars drift slowly sideways across

29
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:17,720
our sky year after year.

30
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:21,920
Gliese 710 hardly drifts at all.

31
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,580
It is coming towards us at about 14

32
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:29,160
kilometers every second, more than 50,000

33
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:31,820
kilometers an hour, and almost none of

34
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:33,640
that motion is sideways.

35
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:36,900
When something barely moves across your

36
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,780
view but keeps getting closer, it is

37
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:43,100
heading for you, like a car on a long,

38
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:46,120
straight road at night. Its headlights do

39
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:49,200
not slide left or right. They just grow.

40
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:52,400
If you saw our film on the nearest star,

41
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:55,780
you met Proxima Centauri about four

42
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:56,720
light-years away.

43
00:01:57,760 --> 00:02:00,200
Proxima is our nearest neighbor today,

44
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:04,500
but stars move, and the title of nearest

45
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:06,040
does not last forever.

46
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:08,240
How do we know where it will be in a

47
00:02:08,280 --> 00:02:10,720
million years? For a long time, we did

48
00:02:10,759 --> 00:02:13,000
not. We were not even sure it would come

49
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:16,220
close at all. In the late 1990s, a

50
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:18,780
satellite called Hipparcos measured the

51
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:21,760
star's position and motion. The best guess

52
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:23,360
then was that it would pass about a

53
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:25,300
light-year from the sun, and the

54
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:29,580
uncertainty was huge. Then came Gaia, the

55
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:31,680
European spacecraft that has measured more

56
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:34,640
than a billion stars. With every new

57
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:37,180
release of its data, the path came into

58
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:40,480
sharper focus, and the star kept coming

59
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:41,040
closer.

60
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:43,780
The latest Gaia measurements put the

61
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:45,720
closest approach at about a fifth of a

62
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:49,220
light-year. That is about 10 to 13,000

63
00:02:49,260 --> 00:02:51,920
times further than Earth is from the sun,

64
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:54,800
five times closer than the old estimate.

65
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:57,940
That still sounds far, so let's shrink it.

66
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:00,540
Make the gap between the sun and Earth one

67
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:04,040
centimeter. Neptune, our outermost planet,

68
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:07,100
is then 30 centimeters away, about the

69
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:11,080
length of a ruler. And Gliese 710 passes

70
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:13,960
more than 100 meters away, the length of a

71
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:14,820
football pitch.

72
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:17,760
So the star will not come anywhere near

73
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:20,420
the planets. But what is out there 100

74
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:22,940
meters from the sun? Something we have

75
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:23,760
never seen.

76
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:26,500
We make one of these every week.

77
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:29,440
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

78
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:32,620
Far beyond the planets, the sun is wrapped

79
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:35,840
in a vast cloud of ice. Trillions of

80
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:38,540
frozen bodies, the leftovers from when the

81
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:41,360
planets formed, drifting so far out that

82
00:03:41,380 --> 00:03:43,180
the sun's pull on them is barely a

83
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:43,680
whisper.

84
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:47,180
It is called the Oort cloud, and it is

85
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:49,180
still one of the great unknowns of our

86
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:52,200
solar system. Nobody has ever seen it

87
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:52,840
directly.

88
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:56,100
We know it is there because of the comets

89
00:03:56,120 --> 00:03:58,600
that fall out of it on journeys that take

90
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:00,580
them hundreds of thousands of years or

91
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:03,880
more. Its inner edge is thought to start a

92
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:06,280
few thousand times further out than Earth.

93
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:09,840
Its outer edge may reach 100,000.

94
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:13,200
Sunlight, which gets to us in eight

95
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:15,600
minutes, would take months to reach the

96
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:19,140
place where Gliese 710 will pass, and that

97
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:21,240
is exactly where the star is going,

98
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:24,000
straight through the Oort cloud.

99
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:27,580
A star passing through the cloud is like a

100
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:29,760
hand brushing through a bowl of floating

101
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:32,900
marbles. Its gravity nudges the comets

102
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:35,760
nearest its path. Some get flung out into

103
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:38,340
the galaxy. Others are knocked onto new

104
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:40,580
orbits that fall in towards the sun.

105
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:44,360
The calculation suggests that Gliese 710

106
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:47,060
will send about 10 extra comets a year,

107
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:50,060
falling in close enough for us to see, and

108
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:52,320
it will keep happening for three to four

109
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:53,520
million years.

110
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:57,660
But here is the strange part. The comets

111
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:00,040
do not arrive while the star is passing.

112
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:01,220
Why?

113
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:04,420
Because they are so far out that falling

114
00:05:04,480 --> 00:05:07,080
in takes hundreds of thousands of years.

115
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:09,820
By the time the shower is at its height,

116
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:12,680
Gliese 710 will already be heading away

117
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:15,460
again, shrinking back into the dark.

118
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:17,620
Should anyone worry?

119
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:21,520
Not much. Most comets that fall in never

120
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:24,120
come close to Earth. Jupiter and Saturn

121
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:26,740
throw many of them back out or steer them

122
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:27,240
away.

123
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:30,820
What the people of that far future get is

124
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:34,040
a sky with more comets in it for a very

125
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:37,700
long time. So what would you see? Imagine

126
00:05:37,820 --> 00:05:39,400
standing on Earth on the night of the

127
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:42,120
closest pass. A new star has been

128
00:05:42,140 --> 00:05:45,300
brightening for thousands of years. Now it

129
00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:47,140
is the brightest star in the whole night

130
00:05:47,240 --> 00:05:49,540
sky, about three times brighter than

131
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:51,940
Sirius and as bright as Jupiter at its

132
00:05:51,980 --> 00:05:54,400
best, and it glows orange.

133
00:05:55,420 --> 00:05:56,860
It will not light up the night like the

134
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:59,580
moon, and it will not warm anyone. It is

135
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:01,500
still billions of times fainter than our

136
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:03,520
sun, but it will move.

137
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:07,600
Most stars look fixed for a lifetime. This

138
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:10,200
one will creep across the sky by about the

139
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:12,960
width of the full moon every 35 years.

140
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:16,820
Then, slowly, it will fade and drift away

141
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:17,300
for good.

142
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:20,260
Is this the first time a star has come

143
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:24,640
this close? Not at all. Gliese 710 will

144
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:26,780
not be the first star to pass this way.

145
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:31,180
About 70,000 years ago, a dim red pair of

146
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:34,600
stars, now called Scholz's Star, skimmed

147
00:06:34,620 --> 00:06:37,040
through the outer Oort cloud less than a

148
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:38,360
light-year from the sun.

149
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:42,080
It was too small and too faint to stir up

150
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:46,220
much. And about 2.8 million years ago, a

151
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:51,160
star much like our sun, called HD 7977,

152
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:54,400
may have passed as close as Gliese 710

153
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:54,700
will.

154
00:06:56,020 --> 00:06:58,200
Gaia's measurements cannot yet pin down

155
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:01,300
exactly how close, so this is not a rare

156
00:07:01,340 --> 00:07:04,560
disaster waiting to happen. It is how life

157
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:05,840
works in a galaxy.

158
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:10,000
The sun is one star among billions, all of

159
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:12,880
them moving, and every few million years,

160
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:14,780
one of them brushes past.

161
00:07:16,860 --> 00:07:18,720
What if the night sky you have always

162
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:22,080
known is not fixed at all, but a slow

163
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:24,720
traffic of stars still on the move?

164
00:07:25,900 --> 00:07:28,160
The bigger question is the cloud itself.

165
00:07:29,180 --> 00:07:31,660
Some astronomers think the sun was born in

166
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:34,320
a crowd of sister stars, and that many of

167
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:36,400
the comets in our Oort cloud were once

168
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:36,980
theirs.

169
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:40,880
If they are right, every star that passes

170
00:07:40,940 --> 00:07:43,080
is part of a very old exchange.

171
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:46,480
Some of the ice falling in from the dark

172
00:07:46,940 --> 00:07:49,340
may have started out around another sun.
